Architecture note: the web component does nav-listening AND DOM scroll ops in one place
(global DOM access). SwiftUI cannot query a global scroll view, so the surface is split into
two pieces sharing one model — the transparent `ScrollRestoration(model:)` companion (mounted
once at the router root, drives the nav seam) + the `.scrollRestoration(model)` modifier (on the
primary ScrollView; saves the offset, applies the restore). This is the faithful native parity.

Leaf-state → web-branch mapping (no fabricated network states for a local utility):
  preparing    ← initial, before first navigation resolves          (loading peer)
  restored     ← POP with a finite saved offset → return to it       (data peer)
  top(fresh)   ← PUSH / REPLACE → start at the top                   (empty peer)
  top(noSaved) ← POP with nothing saved yet → start at the top       (empty peer)
  unavailable  ← sessionStorage disabled → can't persist; still tops (error/offline peer)

